. They widened 30bp to 165bp in Q3, a sharp move that adds about 35 points over the Q1 level.\n- **Issuance:** Norvane priced a C$1.1B bond \"comfortably\" in Q3, so investment-grade debt access is intact.\n- **Equity:** Two utility share offerings were cut back in Q3. Pellham's C$2.4B offering was pulled on 14 October. Equity is the weaker channel, so more of the funding gap falls on debt.\n- **Pellham's response:** It said it will cut 2027 transformer orders by about 40% and resize its Q1 2027 tender. That suggests debt alone doesn't cover its plan at current costs, or that management doesn't want to lever up further.\n\n**Gaps**\n- I found no data on all-in yields, total 2027 capex amounts, debt maturities, or credit metrics such as FFO/debt, ratings or headroom.\n- The capex amounts are said to be in a \"utility tender calendar\", which I didn't find.\n- Norvane is the only confirmed debt deal. One deal doesn't show Pellham can do the same at scale.\n\n**Conclusion:** Norvane-size deals can get done, so capacity exists for the stronger issuers. I can't confirm that debt can fund the full plans. The Pellham cut is the best evidence that it can't without a trade-off. For grid equipment demand, assume lower 2027 volume from Pellham rather than full debt funding.","ttft_ms":3976,"type":"result","duration_ms":34627,"uuid":"cbe9efa6-eb8e-4873-a839-15b87f2b7871","ttft_stream_ms":2489,"time_to_request_ms":58,"time_to_request_phases_ms":{"other":4,"system_prompt":4,"resume_other":0,"process_user_input":3,"input_attachments":12,"input_hooks":1,"conversation_append":1,"transcript_persist":2,"tool_pool_refresh":0,"init_message":5,"init_frame_send":2,"engine_pickup":1,"loop_entry":2,"message_prep":1,"model_config_wait":1,"autocompact":1,"request_announcements":6,"query_setup":0,"model_gates":0,"tool_schema_build":3,"message_normalization":2,"client_creation":2,"request_build":5},"turn_start_resume_kind":"none","process_turn_index":0,"time_to_request_loop_lag_ms":8,"time_to_r